        'Is/is-not analysis, from Kepner and Tregoe’s 1965 method, describes a problem along four lines: what (which part, which defect), where (which machine, which place on the part), when (since when, which shift, which point in the cycle) and how much (how many, trend).\n\nFor each line you write what it is, and what it could be but is not. The “is not” column is where the learning is.',
        'Then ask two questions of every pair. What is distinct about the “is” side? That is a distinction: true only where the problem is. And what changed around the time it started?\n\nA problem that starts on a date almost always has a trigger: a new tool, a program edit, a supplier change, a new operator, a maintenance job.',
        'Stratification is is/is-not with data. Split defects by machine, cavity (one of the part-shaped hollows in a mould; a mould often has 4 or 8), shift, material lot (one delivered batch), operator and week. A flat 5% scrap rate can turn out to be 28% on one cavity and under 2% everywhere else.\n\nConcentrated defects point to a specific cause. Defects spread evenly point to something common: the material, the design or the method.',
        'A true cause must explain every “is” and every “is not”.\n\nTest each theory against the table: “If it is the material, why is line 1 fine?” That one question exposes most weak analyses, because a favourite theory usually explains the defect but not where it is absent.\n\nThis is the step that turns the many candidates on a fishbone into the few worth testing.',
